---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DiceRoller from '@/components/tools/everyday/DiceRoller.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="dice-roller">
  <DiceRoller client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the dice roller</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type dice notation, or tap one of the presets.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Roll</strong>, or press Enter in the notation box.</li>
      <li>
        To roll the same expression many times, set the <strong>×</strong> field. The outcome
        distribution chart then appears below, with the mean and standard deviation of the batch.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The tool shows each die, strikes through dropped dice, and keeps the last forty rolls in the
      history, so you can settle an argument about what you rolled.
    </p>

    <h2>Dice notation</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Notation</th>
            <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
            <th scope="col">Range</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>d20</td><td>One twenty-sided die</td><td>1–20</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3d6</td><td>Three six-sided dice, summed</td><td>3–18</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3d6+2</td><td>Three d6 plus a flat 2</td><td>5–20</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1d20-1</td><td>A d20 with a −1 penalty</td><td>0–19</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2d8+1d6+3</td><td>Mixed dice pools added together</td><td>6–25</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4d6kh3</td><td>Roll four d6, keep the highest three</td><td>3–18</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2d20kh1</td><td>Advantage: roll two d20, keep the higher</td><td>1–20</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2d20kl1</td><td>Disadvantage: keep the lower</td><td>1–20</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1d100</td><td>Percentile die</td><td>1–100</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Up to 500 dice per term and 1,000 sides per die, with as many terms as you need. If the parser
      cannot read part of the input, it explains why in plain English.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the randomness comes from</h2>
    <p>
      Each die reads new entropy from <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, the cryptographically secure
      random number generator of the browser, which the operating system seeds. It does not use
      <code>Math.random</code> or a seed from the clock.
    </p>
    <p>
      The raw values are mapped onto the faces with <strong>rejection sampling</strong>: values in the
      incomplete last block of the 2³² range are discarded and drawn again. A simple remainder would make
      the low-numbered faces very slightly more likely (the classic modulo bias). For a d6, nobody could
      notice the bias, but discarding a value costs nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>Why 3d6 is not the same as 1d18</h2>
    <p>
      Both go from 3 to 18, but they behave very differently. A single die is flat: each face is equally
      likely. The sum of several dice has a bell-shaped distribution, because there are many more ways to
      make a middle total than an extreme one.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Total on 3d6</th>
            <th scope="col">Ways to roll it</th>
            <th scope="col">Probability</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>3 or 18</td><td>1</td><td>0.46%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4 or 17</td><td>3</td><td>1.39%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5 or 16</td><td>6</td><td>2.78%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6 or 15</td><td>10</td><td>4.63%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7 or 14</td><td>15</td><td>6.94%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8 or 13</td><td>21</td><td>9.72%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9 or 12</td><td>25</td><td>11.57%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10 or 11</td><td>27</td><td>12.50%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The 216 combinations add up: a 10 or an 11 is twenty-seven times as likely as a 3. Roll a batch of a
      few hundred here, and the chart draws that curve.
    </p>

    <h2>Averages worth knowing</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Dice</th>
            <th scope="col">Average</th>
            <th scope="col">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1d4</td><td>2.5</td><td>Any single die averages (sides + 1) ÷ 2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1d6</td><td>3.5</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>1d8</td><td>4.5</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>1d10</td><td>5.5</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>1d12</td><td>6.5</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>1d20</td><td>10.5</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3d6</td><td>10.5</td><td>Same average as 1d20, much tighter spread</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4d6 keep highest 3</td><td>≈12.24</td><td>The classic ability-score roll</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2d20 keep highest</td><td>≈13.83</td><td>Advantage: about +3.3 on average</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2d20 keep lowest</td><td>≈7.17</td><td>Disadvantage</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Keep-highest and keep-lowest have no simple closed-form average, so the tool shows the exact range for
      them and leaves the expected value to a batch simulation. Roll 4d6kh3 a thousand times here, and the
      mean comes very close to 12.24.
    </p>

    <h2>Uses beyond tabletop games</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Board games with missing dice</li>
      <li>Classroom probability lessons: batch a thousand rolls and show the distribution as it forms</li>
      <li>Random selection when a coin flip has too few outcomes</li>
      <li>Simulation and playtesting of homebrew game mechanics</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      No server sees your rolls. The history stays in the page and is deleted when you close the tab or
      press Clear.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
